DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       rraawwttooppppmm  reads raw RGB bytes as input and produces a PPM image as out‐
       put.  The input file is just RGB bytes.  You have to specify the  width
       and  height  on the command line, since the program obviously can't get
       them from the file.  rraawwttooppppmm assumes the maxval of the  input  samples
       is 255, and makes the maxval of the output PPM 255.

       rraawwttooppppmm  assumes  the  pixels  come top first in the input stream.  If
       they are actually bottom first, the resulting PPM is  upside  down,  so
       run it through ppaammfflliipp --ttbb.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), rraawwttooppppmm  recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --hheeaaddeerrsskkiipp
              Skip  over this many bytes at the beginning of the input stream.
              Use this option when the input has some kind of header  followed
              by a raster suitable for rraawwttooppppmm.


       --rroowwsskkiipp
              Skip  this  many  bytes  at  the  end of each row of the raster.
              (Some input streams have padding at the end of rows).


       --rrggbb --rrbbgg --ggrrbb --ggbbrr --bbrrgg --bbggrr
              This option specifies the order of the color components for each
              pixel.  The default is --rrggbb.


       --iinntteerrppiixxeell --iinntteerrrrooww
              These  options  specify how the colors are interleaved.  The de‐
              fault is --iinntteerrppiixxeell, meaning interleaved by pixel.  A  byte  of
              red,  a byte of green, and a byte of blue, or whatever color or‐
              der you specified.  --iinntteerrrrooww means interleaved by row -  a  row
              of  red,  a  row  of green, a row of blue, assuming standard rgb
              color order.  An --iinntteerrppllaannee option - all the red  pixels,  then
              all  the  green,  then all the blue - would be an obvious exten‐
              sion, but is not implemented.  You could get the same effect  by
              splitting  the file into three parts (perhaps using dddd), turning
              each part into a PGM file with rraawwttooppggmm, and then combining them
              with rrggbb33ttooppppmm.




